Parses a decimal integer from a string: an optional minus sign, then one or more digits. Anything else raises a named error, with a message that says what was wrong.
The probe is named errors on real input. Each failure is a raise with its message, so the outcome is error and the message says why. A minus sign alone, and an empty string, are both errors.
---
(lam s
  (letrec digits (lam l (lam acc
        (if (? l) acc
            (let c (head l)
              (if (< c 48) (raise "not a number")
                  (if (< 57 c) (raise "not a number")
                      ((digits (tail l)) (+ (* acc 10) (- c 48)))))))))
    (if (? s) (raise "empty")
        (if (= (head s) 45)
            (if (? (tail s)) (raise "no digits") (- 0 ((digits (tail s)) 0)))
            ((digits s) 0)))))
---
"42" => 42 fuel 200000
"-123" => -123 fuel 200000
"0" => 0 fuel 200000
"007" => 7 fuel 200000
"99999" => 99999 fuel 200000
"-0" => 0 fuel 200000
"" => error fuel 200000
"-" => error fuel 200000
"12a" => error fuel 200000
"1 2" => error fuel 200000
"+5" => error fuel 200000
